WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.840
In the summer of 1973, pollsters Lou
Harris and George Gallup began asking

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.250
people whether President Richard Nixon
should be impeached. At that early point

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.449
in the Watergate saga, most people did
not want to impeach the president. Public

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.519
opinion turned against Nixon in the late
spring of 1974, and Nixon resigned in

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:28.560
August. Now, Congressman Brad Sherman,
Democrat from California, has filed an

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.090
impeachment resolution against Donald
Trump using language that is similar to

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.750
the resolutions language used during
Watergate. The measure has only one

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.460
co-sponsor, and is unlikely to gain much
traction at this point. What do the polls

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.750
say? A July poll from Monmouth University
is pretty much in line with the small

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:51.059
handful of polls we have on the subject.
In it, 41% say President Trump should be

00:00:51.059 --> 00:00:56.370
impeached and compelled to leave office,
while 53% say that he should not be. In

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:01.260
our deeply divided political environment,
large majorities of Democrats want to

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.739
impeach him; large majorities of
Republicans don't. A third of

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.700
independents say he should go.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.360
This is a sensitive issue with serious political ramifications.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.300
Let us know what you think
and let us know your comments. Also, let

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.160
us know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:24.049
sure to subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

